    "durationMs": 122429,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified.\n\n**What I built:**\n- `src/app.js` — Express 5 app with all five routes: `POST /links` (201 with `{code, url, clicks: 0}`, 409 on duplicate custom code, 422 on malformed JSON/invalid url/invalid code), `GET /links` (ascending creation order, `{items, total}`), `GET /links/:code` (200/404), `GET /r/:code` (302 + Location, atomic single-increment via `UPDATE ... SET clicks = clicks + 1 RETURNING url`), and a JSON 404 fallback. URLs are validated with WHATWG `new URL` (http/https, non-empty host); codes via `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}$`; auto-codes are 7 random chars with collision retry.\n- `src/db.js` — pg Pool reading `DATABASE_URL` from the environment.\n- `migrations/001-create-links.js` — node-pg-migrate migration creating the `links` table with unique code constraint.\n- `start.sh` — applies pending migrations then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- `README.md` — startup instructions.\n\n**Verification:** a 40-assertion smoke suite passed covering every requirement, including edge cases (empty/missing url, `ftp://`, `http://`, 4/20-char code boundaries, malformed JSON, unknown codes). I also restarted the server and confirmed links, click counts, and creation order persist, and that migrations are idempotent (\"No migrations to run!\" on the second start). The test data was truncated afterward so the database is clean for the grader."
  }
